    64	### 2.1 Step 0 is correct; the "wrong category" objection is refuted
    65	
    66	Grok-xpoll §3.1 asserts that the integral closure `B` of `A = C[F_1,F_2]` in `K`
    67	"need not sit inside `C[x,y]`", so that `A^2 -> Spec B` may fail to be a
    68	morphism. That is false, for a one-line reason the attack omits:
    69	
    70	> **Lemma 2.1.** `A ⊆ B ⊆ C[x,y]`.
    71	> *Proof.* Let `f ∈ B`. Then `f ∈ K` is integral over `A`, hence integral over
    72	> the larger ring `C[x,y]`. `C[x,y]` is integrally closed in its fraction field
    73	> `K`. Hence `f ∈ C[x,y]`. ∎
    74	
    75	So `Y := Spec B` receives a morphism `j: A^2 -> Y`, `q: Y -> A^2` is finite
    76	(Noether, `B` is a finite `A`-module), `Y` is normal, and `q ∘ j = F`. Since `F`
    77	is quasi-finite, so is `j`; `j` is birational; `Y` is normal; hence by **Zariski's
    78	Main Theorem** (Mumford, *Red Book* III.9; Grothendieck's form EGA IV 8.12.6:
    79	`j` factors as an open immersion into a finite `Z -> Y`, and `O(Z) ⊆ C[x,y]` is
    80	integral over `B` which is integrally closed in `K`, so `Z = Y`) `j` is an **open
    81	immersion**. `q` is **flat** by miracle flatness (Matsumura, *CRT* Thm 23.1: `Y`
    82	normal surface ⟹ CM by Serre; base regular; fibres of dimension 0).
    83	
    84	> **Lemma 2.2.** `B_Y := Y \ U` is nonempty and pure of codimension one.
    85	> *Proof.* Nonempty: if `U = Y` then `q` is finite étale (it is étale on `U`
    86	> because `F = q∘j` is étale and `j` is an open immersion) over the simply
    87	> connected `A^2`, so `d = 1`. Purity: let `W` be a component of `Y \ U` of
    88	> codimension `>= 2`. Pick a basic open `D(f) ⊆ Y` meeting `W` and no other
    89	> component of `Y \ U`. `D(f)` is affine normal, `D(f) ∩ U = D_U(f|_U)` is a
    90	> basic open of the affine `U`, hence affine, and `D(f) \ (D(f)∩U) = D(f)∩W` has
    91	> codimension `>= 2`. For a normal Noetherian domain `R`, `R = ∩_{ht p = 1} R_p`
    92	> (Matsumura Thm 11.5), so removing a closed set of codimension `>= 2` does not
    93	> change global functions: `O(D(f)∩U) = O(D(f))`. Two affines with the same
    94	> coordinate ring linked by an open immersion are equal, so `D(f)∩W = ∅`. ∎
    95	
    96	> **Lemma 2.3.** `A_F = q(B_Y)`, and it is a curve.
    97	> *Proof.* `⊆`: if `x_n -> ∞` in `A^2` with `F(x_n) -> p`, then `q` proper puts
    98	> `x_n` eventually in a compact of `Y`; a limit `y` has `q(y)=p`, and `y ∉ U`
    99	> since `x_n` leaves every compact of `U`. `⊇`: `U` is analytically dense in the
   100	> irreducible `Y`, so any `y ∈ B_Y` is a limit of `y_n ∈ U`; these leave every
   101	> compact of `U`, and `q(y_n) -> q(y)`. Finally `q` is finite, so no component of
   102	> `B_Y` is contracted: `q(B_Y)` is closed and pure of dimension 1. ∎
   103	
   104	Lemma 2.3 *reproves*, in this case, Jelonek's hypersurface theorem (Jelonek,
   105	*The set of points at which a polynomial map is not proper*, Ann. Polon. Math.
   106	**58** (1993) 259–266), so (E) does not consume it as an external input.
   107	
   108	**Disposition.** Step 0 stands. Grok-xpoll's inference that `CANON-SIEVE/Y`, the
   109	free class group `Cl(Y) =~ Z^{m_Y}`, `kappa-bar(Y) = -infty` and the row-29
   110	reopen "inherit Step 0" and die is therefore **withdrawn as a reason**: those
   111	clients ride a correct construction. (Their own merits are outside this gate;
   112	`Cl(Y)` free follows from `C[Y]^* ⊆ C[x,y]^* = C^*` and the excision sequence,
   113	and `kappa-bar(Y) = -infty` from Iitaka's monotonicity `kappa-bar(U) >= kappa-bar(Y)`
   114	for `U ⊆ Y` open — Iitaka, *Algebraic Geometry* GTM 76, §11.)
   115	
   116	### 2.2 The fibre decomposition and the four boxes
   117	
   118	`q` is finite flat of degree `d`, so for **every** `p ∈ A^2`
   119	`sum_{y ∈ q^{-1}(p)} e_y = d`, where `e_y := dim_C O_{Y,y} ⊗ κ(p)` is the local
   120	multiplicity. Each `y ∈ q^{-1}(p)` sorts into one of four boxes by
   121	(center in `U` vs. in `B_Y = Y-U`) × (`e_y = 1` vs. `e_y > 1`). At a generic
   122	`p ∈ D_0`, with `B_1,...,B_{m_Y}` the components of `B_Y`,
   123	`delta_j := deg(B_j -> D)` and `e_j := ord_{B_j}(q^*h)` (`h` = reduced equation
   124	of `D`), the boxes are:
   125	
   126	| | `e_y = 1` | `e_y > 1` |
   127	|---|---|---|
   128	| **center in `U`** | count `a = #F^{-1}(p)`. **NONEMPTY**, `a >= 1` | **EMPTY** |
   129	| **center in `Y-U`** | count `b := sum_{j: e_j = 1} delta_j` | count `sum_{j: e_j>=2} delta_j`. **NONEMPTY** |
   130	
   131	with `d = a + b + sum_{j: e_j>=2} delta_j e_j` and (§3) `sigma = a + b`.
   132	
   133	**Box `(U, e>1)` is EMPTY — this is exactly where "Keller" enters.** `F` étale
   134	⟹ `q|_U` étale ⟹ unramified ⟹ every fibre point in `U` is reduced with residue
   135	field `C`, i.e. `e_y = 1`. This is the only box emptied by the hypothesis on the
   136	*source*; everything else is target-side or global.
   137	
   138	**Box `(U, e=1)` is NONEMPTY, i.e. `a >= 1`.** Let `h_i` be the equation of a
   139	component `D_i` of `D`. `h_i ∘ F` is non-constant (otherwise `F(A^2)` lies in a
   140	curve, contradicting dominance), so `E_i := F^{-1}(D_i) = V(h_i∘F)` is a nonempty
   141	curve. `F` is quasi-finite, so `F(E_i) ⊆ D_i` is constructible of dimension one,
   142	hence dense in `D_i`. So `#F^{-1}(p) >= 1` for generic `p ∈ D_i`. **No hypothesis
   143	used** — in particular this is block-free and reducibility-tolerant.
   144	
   145	**Box `(Y-U, e>1)` is NONEMPTY.** `q` is étale over `A^2 \ D` (its fibres there
   146	lie in `U` by Lemma 2.3, where `F` is étale) and étale at every point of `U`. If
   147	`q` were unramified at the generic point of every `B_j`, its ramification locus
   148	would be a proper closed subset of `B_Y`, hence finite, and its branch locus
   149	finite; **purity of the branch locus** (Zariski–Nagata; Nagata, Illinois J. Math.
   150	**3** (1959) 328–333; SGA1 Exp. X — `A^2` regular, `Y` normal, `q` finite) forces
   151	the branch locus to be pure of codimension one or empty, hence empty, hence `q`
   152	finite étale over the simply connected `A^2`, hence `d = 1`. Contradiction. So
   153	**some `e_j >= 2`**, and `sum_{j: e_j>=2} delta_j e_j >= 2`.
   154	
   155	**Box `(Y-U, e=1)` is the whole gate.** It is nonempty exactly when some
   156	dicritical is *unramified* over its image. Its count is Sol-xpoll's `b`. §4
   157	settles it for `d <= 3` and reduces it at `d = 4` to a single alternative.
   158	
   159	### 2.3 Two free consequences of the box audit
   160	
   161	> **Prop 2.4.** `a <= d - 2` and `sigma <= d - 2`, for every component of `A_F`
   162	> lying in the branch locus of `q`.
   163	> *Proof.* `sigma = a + b = d - sum_{j: e_j >= 2} delta_j e_j <= d - 2`, and
   164	> `a <= sigma`. ∎
   165	
   166	This is the load-bearing repair. Sol-xpoll wrote that with `sigma` replaced by
   167	`a`, "`a - a_p = d-1` is compatible with `a = d-1, a_p = 0`". It is not: `a` obeys
   168	the same `<= d-2` ceiling, proved directly and without any appeal to `g != 1`.
   169	
   170	> **Prop 2.5.** Any Keller counterexample has `d >= 3`.
   171	> *Proof.* Some component `D_1 ⊆ A_F` is a branch component (previous paragraph).
   172	> Over it, `1 <= a^{(1)} <= d-2`. ∎
   173	
   174	Consistency check, not a new theorem: `d = 2` forces `K/Frac A` Galois, and the
   175	Galois case of JC is classical (Campbell, *A condition for a polynomial map to be
   176	invertible*, Math. Ann. **205** (1973) 243–248). The box audit reproduces it.
   177	
