   456	## 5. The corrected identity, its proof, and the monodromy cycle type
   457	
   458	**Theorem 5.1 (corrected missing-multiplicity identity).**  Let `S` be a
   459	smooth affine surface, `F:S->C^2` a dominant etale morphism of geometric
   460	degree `N`, `A_F` its nonproper-value set, `B_i` an irreducible component of
   461	`A_F`.  Then for all `z` in a Zariski-open dense subset of `B_i`,
   462	
   463	```text
   464	N  =  f(z)  +  sum_{l in L_F : Phi(l)=closure(B_i)}  s_l * mu_l.   (2.3')
   465	```
   466	
   467	*Proof.*  Take `z in B_i - Z_i` with `Z_i` the finite set of Lemma 2.2,
   468	enlarged by the finitely many points excluded in Corollary 4.2 for each of
   469	the finitely many `l` over `B_i`.  By Lemma 2.2(i) the fibre `Phi^{-1}(z)`
   470	is finite, so Corollary 3.2 applies and gives
   471	`N = f(z) + sum_{p in Phi^{-1}(z) cap D} mult_p(Phi)`.  By Lemma 2.2(ii)
   472	that boundary sum decomposes over the dicritical lines whose image is
   473	`closure(B_i)`, and by Corollary 4.2 the contribution of each such `l` is
   474	`s_l*mu_l`.  QED
   475	
   476	**Corollary 5.2 ((2.3) is exactly the `s=1` case).**  (2.3) holds at a
   477	generic point of `B_i` iff every dicritical over `B_i` is birational onto
   478	its image (`s_l=1`).  In general it fails one-sidedly:
   479	`f(z) <= N - sum_l mu_l`, with equality iff all `s_l=1`.
   480	
   481	The following proposition is the structural content that makes the charged
   482	application work, and it is what the ledger's Lemma 2.3 was implicitly
   483	reaching for.
   484	
   485	**Proposition 5.3 (inertia cycle type).**  Keep the hypotheses of
   486	Theorem 5.1 and let `z in B_i` be generic.  Let `delta` be a meridian of
   487	`B_i` at `z` (the boundary of a small disc transverse to `B_i` at `z`,
   488	inside a ball meeting `A_F` only in `B_i`).  The monodromy of the
   489	`N`-sheeted covering `F : S - F^{-1}(A_F) -> C^2 - A_F` along `delta` is a
   490	permutation of cycle type
   491	
   492	```text
   493	{ mu_l  repeated  s_l  times : l in L_F over B_i }  union  { 1^{f(z)} }.
   494	                                                                   (5.1)
   495	```
   496	
   497	*Proof.*  Choose a ball `W ni z` as in Lemma 3.1, small enough that
   498	`W cap A_F = W cap B_i` is a smooth disc and `Phi^{-1}(W) -> W` is finite of
   499	degree `N`.  Then `Phi^{-1}(W)` is the disjoint union of connected open
   500	neighbourhoods `V_p` of the points `p in Phi^{-1}(z)`, with
   501	`V_p -> W` finite of degree `mult_p(Phi)`.
   502	
   503	For `p in F^{-1}(z)`: `F` is etale at `p`, `mult_p=1`, so `V_p -> W` is an
   504	isomorphism and `V_p subset S`; it is a single sheet, fixed by the
   505	monodromy, contributing a `1`-cycle.  There are `f(z)` of these.
   506	
